## Migration: Thread.Abort → Cooperative Cancellation (.NET 8)

### Pattern Classification

| Usage | Pattern | Replacement |
|-------|---------|-------------|
| `while (_running)` loop | Cancellable work loop | `CancellationToken` |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Control flow cleanup | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Continue after abort | `token.IsCancellationRequested` check |
| `_workerThread.Abort()` | Force stop | `CancellationTokenSource.Cancel()` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private Thread? _workerThread;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        var token = _cts.Token;
        _workerThread = new Thread(() => ProcessLoop(token));
        _workerThread.IsBackground = true;
        _workerThread.Start();
    }

    private void ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = GetNextItem(token); // pass token to unblock
                ProcessItem(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Clean up partial work — mirrors the old ThreadAbortException block
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                // No ResetAbort needed — we simply continue to the loop condition,
                // which will exit because the token is cancelled.
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        if (_cts is null) return;

        _cts.Cancel(); // signal cooperative cancellation

        // Wait up to 2 seconds for graceful exit
        _workerThread?.Join(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(2000));

        // If thread is still alive, there is no Thread.Abort equivalent.
        // The thread will exit at its next cancellation checkpoint.
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts?.Dispose();
        _cts = null;
    }
}
```

### Key Changes to `GetNextItem`

If `GetNextItem` blocks (e.g., on a queue), it **must** accept and respect the token:

```csharp
// Before: blocks indefinitely, only Thread.Abort could interrupt
private WorkItem GetNextItem() => _queue.Take();

// After: throws OperationCanceledException when token fires
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token) => _queue.Take(token);
// BlockingCollection<T>.Take(CancellationToken) supports this natively.
```

If using `Thread.Sleep` inside `GetNextItem` as a polling delay:
```csharp
// Before:
Thread.Sleep(500);

// After:
token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(500); // returns immediately if cancelled
```

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Why it matters |
|---------|---------------|
| **Not passing `token` to blocking calls** | Cancellation only works if code cooperates. Without it, `Stop()` hangs until the blocking call returns naturally. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers/callbacks. Always `Dispose()`. |
| **Expecting instant stop** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation only takes effect at the next checkpoint. Ensure checkpoints are frequent. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException` broadly** | Use `when (token.IsCancellationRequested)` filter to only catch *your* cancellation, not unrelated ones. |
| **`Thread.Join` without timeout** | If the worker ignores cancellation, `Join()` blocks forever. Always use a timeout. |

### If `GetNextItem` Cannot Be Modified

If the blocking call is in unmodifiable code (e.g., third-party library), move it to a child process and use `Process.Kill()` as the last resort — this is the only reliable way to forcibly terminate uncooperative code in modern .NET.
